
module moodle_types is

(* ===========================================================================
   Moodle — abstract and concrete domains.

   Rebuilt 2026-08-24 around the HYBRID EIGHT disruptions. The previous set was
   eight variants of "a write fails", which is Moodle's STRONGEST area:
   quiz_attempt::process_finish wraps its writes in start_delegated_transaction
   and rolls back on exception, so those faults produce a clean error page and
   a PASS. A suite aimed there cannot discriminate.

   The new set aims at seams verified to be weak by reading the SUT:
     * mod/assign/locallib.php save_submission() has NO delegated transaction —
       the only ones in that file wrap get_user_submission/get_group_submission,
       which create the submission ROW, not its CONTENT. So the status row and
       the onlinetext row are written non-atomically.
     * process_save_grade() has no submission-changed check. Submission EDITING
       has one ($string['submissionmodified']); grading does not.
     * grade_item->needsupdate defaults to 1 and recompute is deferred via
       grade_regrade_final_grades_if_required(), so a course total can be read
       stale.
     * \mod_quiz\task\update_overdue_attempts is a scheduled_task: Moodle
       depends on cron for CORRECTNESS here, not merely timeliness.

   APP1_WRITE_FAIL is retained deliberately as an EXPECTED-PASS CONTROL. A
   sweep in which everything fails is exactly as suspect as one in which
   nothing does; a control the SUT passes is what makes the failures credible.
   =========================================================================== *)

(* ONE of each, because the seeded fixture has exactly one of each: course
   id 2 (MOD), quiz cmid 1, assign cmid 2. Modelling three courses and four
   assignments was not conservatism, it was unfaithfulness — it multiplied the
   composed state space to 6.9M without expressing a single behaviour the SUT
   can exhibit. Widen these only when the fixture actually widens. *)
type CourseId is
    crs_0
with ==
end type

type QuizId is
    qz_0
with ==
end type

(* ONE attempt. None of the eight faults needs a second: INFRA_CRON_DEAD is
   about a single attempt never leaving IN_PROGRESS, not about concurrency
   between attempts. A second value doubled the composed state space and bought
   no expressible behaviour. Widen if a purpose ever needs a re-attempt. *)
type AttemptId is
    att_0
with ==
end type

type AssignId is
    asn_0
with ==
end type

(* An ENUMERATION, not Grade(val : Nat).
   Nat is unbounded, and output gates receive `?g' freely, so CADP enumerated
   naturals: the SI alone reached 1617 labels and the composition 1.57M states.
   Two abstract values are all the properties need — a full mark, and the zero
   a stale or unrecomputed total shows. That is what an abstract domain is for. *)
type Grade is
    GRADE_FULL,
    GRADE_ZERO
with ==
end type

(* The lifecycle of a quiz attempt AS THE STUDENT CAN SEE IT.

   ABANDONED is new and is the whole point of INFRA_CRON_DEAD: when a quiz has
   a time limit and overduehandling=autosubmit, an attempt left open past its
   deadline is closed by \mod_quiz\task\update_overdue_attempts. If cron never
   runs, it stays IN_PROGRESS forever and the student's work is never graded.
   Without a terminal value to require, that fault has nothing to violate. *)
(* Exactly the three states the quiz page actually RENDERS, one abstract value
   per distinct string. GRADED and FAILED were removed:

     GRADED  rendered the same "Finished" as SUBMITTED, so the oracle could not
             tell them apart — an ambiguous mapping is an unusable one. The
             grade itself is observed through GRADE_PERSISTED, not here.
     FAILED  had NO rendering at all. Nothing in Moodle displays it, so any
             oracle requiring it could never match: the same vacuous-oracle
             defect as the old unobservable `ver' parameter. *)
type AttemptStatus is
    IN_PROGRESS,     (* "In progress"     *)
    SUBMITTED,       (* "Finished"        *)
    ABANDONED        (* "Never submitted" *)
with ==
end type

(* Assignment submission status, DISTINCT from AttemptStatus.

   Reusing one type for both conflated two different lifecycles and hid the
   fault APP_PARTIAL_SUBMISSION is about.

   DRAFT WAS REMOVED, with the fixture change that made it unrenderable.
   The assignment now sets submissiondrafts=0, so a single save writes the
   status AND the content -- which is the non-atomic operation
   APP_PARTIAL_SUBMISSION is about. Under submissiondrafts=1 the two were
   separate user actions, the modelled operation did not exist in the SUT, and
   the property was unreachable by any concrete path.
   Measured rendering, submissiondrafts=0:
     NO_SUBMISSION  "No submissions have been made yet"
     SUBMITTED_FG   "Submitted for grading"
   There is no third state to observe: with drafts off Moodle never renders
   "Draft (not submitted)", so keeping DRAFT would put a value in the domain
   that no page can produce -- the vacuous-oracle defect this model has been
   cleaned of twice already. *)
type SubmissionStatus is
    NO_SUBMISSION,
    SUBMITTED_FG
with ==
end type

(* WHO is currently driving the browser.

   The specification has TWO actors -- STUDENT and TEACHER -- and OPEN_GRADING
   / GRADE_SUBMISSION are the teacher's. An SI with one implicit session cannot
   drive them at all: as student1, /mod/assign/view.php?action=grade hits
   require_login() and returns a Notice page, so the strongest property in the
   suite (APP_GRADE_STALE_SUB) would have timed out on wait_for and been
   recorded INCONCLUSIVE -- a tester defect wearing a verdict.

   Authentication belongs in the SI because the SI IS the function
   I : D_abs -> D_sys: logging in is how an abstract action is EXECUTED against
   this system. It is a system action in Act_sys, and the rule "each abstract
   input is preceded by an input from Act_sys" is exactly what a login sequence
   satisfies. Doing it out of band would also make UE2_SESSION_EXPIRE
   inexpressible -- the fault kills the session, and what must follow (the
   login page, the timeout message, re-authentication) would be invisible to
   the model.

   NONE is the initial state and is also where SESSION_ERROR_SHOWN returns us:
   that is precisely what the session-expiry fault does. *)
type Actor is
    NONE,
    AS_STUDENT,
    AS_TEACHER
with ==
end type

(* Which revision of the submitted text is stored/displayed.

   PLACEHOLDER is the first draft, FINAL the student's update. The stale-read
   properties turn entirely on this: APP_PARTIAL_SUBMISSION asks whether the
   STATUS can advance while the CONTENT stays PLACEHOLDER, and
   APP_GRADE_STALE_SUB asks whether a grade can be confirmed against a version
   the teacher never saw.

   Note this is now OBSERVABLE, which it was not before. The old model carried
   `ver' on CONFIRMED_GRADE with no concrete counterpart — the tester read a
   grade cell containing "100.00" and nothing that distinguished the versions —
   so the oracle either skipped the check (a vacuous pass) or could not match.
   It is now read from the submission TEXT, which the SUT genuinely renders. *)
type SubmissionVersion is
    PLACEHOLDER,
    FINAL
with ==
end type


(* ---- Abstract gate channels ---------------------------------------------
   INPUT gates carry values the tester supplies; OUTPUT gates carry values the
   SUT supplies. The split is declared for TESTOR in testor/moodle.io. *)

channel EnrolChannel is
    (crs : CourseId)
end channel

channel ViewQuizChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, qz : QuizId)
end channel

channel AttemptChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, qz : QuizId, att : AttemptId)
end channel

channel SaveChannel is
    (att : AttemptId)
end channel

channel SubmitChannel is
    (att : AttemptId)
end channel

channel GradeChannel is
    (att : AttemptId, g : Grade)
end channel

(* OUTPUT. The attempt lifecycle state the quiz page shows. Oracle for
   INFRA_CRON_DEAD and DB_ATTEMPT_STEP_LOSS. *)
channel AttemptStateChannel is
    (att : AttemptId, st : AttemptStatus)
end channel

channel ViewAssignChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, asn : AssignId)
end channel

(* Student submit / update actions. *)
channel AssignChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, asn : AssignId, ver : SubmissionVersion)
end channel

channel UpdateChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, asn : AssignId, ver : SubmissionVersion)
end channel

(* OUTPUT, SPLIT INTO TWO GATES ON PURPOSE.
   The concretizer performs ONE `observe' per gate and can capture ONE
   parameter, so a gate carrying two freely-observed values is unsupported:
   the second `observe' is silently dropped by si_lnt_parser (it guards on
   `observe is None'), leaving that parameter with no observation at all. A
   single SUBMISSION_STATE(asn, ?st, ?ver) therefore made `ver' -- the whole
   stale-read property -- vacuous, in exactly the way the old unobservable
   `ver' on CONFIRMED_GRADE was.

   Split, each gate has one free parameter and one observation, and the
   counter-example becomes a TRACE property: the specification must never
   offer SUBMISSION_STATUS(SUBMITTED_FG) followed by SUBMISSION_TEXT(PLACEHOLDER)
   with no intervening status. That is checkable by model checking, which the
   conjunction inside one label was not. *)
channel SubmissionStatusChannel is
    (asn : AssignId, st : SubmissionStatus)
end channel

channel SubmissionTextChannel is
    (asn : AssignId, ver : SubmissionVersion)
end channel

(* Teacher opens the grading form (INPUT). *)
channel OpenGradingChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, asn : AssignId)
end channel

(* OUTPUT. Which version the grading form displays to the teacher. Oracle for
   APP_GRADE_STALE_SUB: this is what the teacher actually judged. *)
channel GradingShowsChannel is
    (asn : AssignId, ver : SubmissionVersion)
end channel

(* Teacher awards a grade (INPUT). Carries the grade, not the version: the
   teacher chooses the mark, the SYSTEM decides which version it lands on. *)
channel GradeActionChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, asn : AssignId, g : Grade)
end channel

(* OUTPUT. The confirmed grade AND the version it was awarded against. *)
channel ConfirmedGradeChannel is
    (asn : AssignId, g : Grade, ver : SubmissionVersion)
end channel

(* Student opens the course total (INPUT) / the total shown (OUTPUT).
   Oracle for APP_LAZY_REGRADE. *)
channel ViewTotalChannel is
    (crs : CourseId)
end channel

channel CourseTotalChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, g : Grade)
end channel

(* Internal APP <-> DATABASE handshake. Hidden in compose_moodle.lnt: a
   browser cannot see these, so leaving them visible would let TESTOR build
   purposes around events the tester can neither stimulate nor observe. *)
channel WriteAttemptChannel is
    (crs : CourseId, qz : QuizId, att : AttemptId)
end channel

channel StatusChannel is
    (att : AttemptId, s : AttemptStatus)
end channel

channel WriteSubmissionChannel is
    (asn : AssignId, ver : SubmissionVersion)
end channel

(* Deliberately SPLIT into the status write and the content write, because
   Moodle performs them as two unprotected writes. Modelling them as one gate
   would make APP_PARTIAL_SUBMISSION inexpressible. *)
channel StatusWriteChannel is
    (asn : AssignId, st : SubmissionStatus)
end channel

channel ContentWriteChannel is
    (asn : AssignId, ver : SubmissionVersion)
end channel


(* ================================================================
   CONCRETE DOMAIN  D_sys  (for the System Interface)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------
   Formal types for the HTML/HTTP interaction layer. The interpretation
   I(L) mapping these enums to runtime URLs/XPaths/strings lives in
   EVALUATION/moodle/properties/concrete_domain.yml and is consumed by the
   HTMLExecutor via framework/concretization/si_lnt_parser.py.

   Moodle is server-rendered and fully URL-addressable, so the SI uses
   direct navigation rather than a screen state machine.
   ================================================================ *)

type Route is
    rt_dashboard,
    rt_course,
    rt_enrol,
    rt_quiz_view,
    rt_quiz_attempt,
    rt_quiz_summary,
    rt_quiz_review,
    rt_assign_view,
    rt_assign_edit,
    rt_assign_grader,
    rt_course_total,
    -- Authentication. rt_logout is /login/logout.php WITHOUT a sesskey, which
    -- Moodle answers with a confirmation page carrying a Continue button --
    -- verified live. The sesskey-bearing URL cannot be used because the key is
    -- per-session and the SI has no way to carry a runtime value into a route.
    rt_login,
    rt_logout
with ==
end type

type Selector is
    -- Enrolment / course
    sel_enrol_btn,
    sel_course_heading,
    -- Quiz attempt flow
    sel_attempt_quiz_btn,
    sel_start_attempt_confirm,
    sel_quiz_question,
    sel_quiz_answer_input,
    sel_finish_attempt_link,
    sel_submit_all_btn,
    -- Union selectors for PROCESS_ATTEMPT's DB_ATTEMPT_STEP_LOSS handling --
    -- see system_interface_moodle.lnt and concrete_domain.yml for why these
    -- are unions rather than a LOTOS `alt' between two plain selectors.
    sel_finish_attempt_anchor,
    sel_finish_attempt_click_target,
    sel_submit_confirm_btn,
    sel_quiz_grade_value,
    -- The attempt-state cell on the quiz view page ("In progress",
    -- "Finished", "Never submitted"). Oracle for INFRA_CRON_DEAD.
    sel_attempt_state_cell,
    -- Assignment submission flow
    sel_add_submission_btn,
    sel_edit_submission_btn,
    sel_online_text_editor,
    sel_save_changes_btn,
    sel_submit_for_grading_btn,
    sel_submission_status,
    -- The STORED submission text as rendered back to the student. This is what
    -- makes SubmissionVersion observable at all.
    sel_submission_text,
    -- Teacher grading
    sel_grade_input,
    sel_grade_save_btn,
    -- The submission text as shown INSIDE the grading form — what the teacher
    -- actually judged. Oracle for APP_GRADE_STALE_SUB.
    sel_grading_submission_text,
    sel_stale_warning,
    -- Grade outputs
    sel_assign_grade_cell,
    sel_course_total_cell,
    -- Surfaced errors
    sel_connection_error,
    sel_session_error,
    sel_write_error,
    -- Authentication controls. All verified live on /login/index.php.
    sel_username,
    sel_password,
    sel_login_btn,
    sel_logout_confirm,
    -- The user menu only renders when a session exists, so waiting on it is
    -- what confirms the login actually took.
    sel_user_menu
with ==
end type

type ConcreteValue is
    cv_quiz_answer,
    cv_assign_text,       -- the PLACEHOLDER revision
    cv_assign_update,     -- the FINAL revision
    cv_grade_100,
    cv_grade_0,
    -- Credentials, seeded by seed_moodle.php.
    cv_student_user, cv_student_pass,
    cv_teacher_user, cv_teacher_pass
with ==
end type

(* ---- Translation function L : D_abs -> D_sys ---- *)
function L_grade (g : Grade) : ConcreteValue is
    case g in
        GRADE_FULL -> return cv_grade_100
      | GRADE_ZERO -> return cv_grade_0
    end case
end function

(* Which concrete text belongs to which abstract revision. This is the pair the
   oracle compares against what the SUT renders, and it is why
   SubmissionVersion is now checkable rather than provenance-only. *)
function L_version (ver : SubmissionVersion) : ConcreteValue is
    case ver in
        PLACEHOLDER -> return cv_assign_text
      | FINAL       -> return cv_assign_update
    end case
end function

(* Which credentials belong to which actor. *)
function L_user (a : Actor) : ConcreteValue is
    case a in
        AS_TEACHER -> return cv_teacher_user
      | any        -> return cv_student_user
    end case
end function

function L_pass (a : Actor) : ConcreteValue is
    case a in
        AS_TEACHER -> return cv_teacher_pass
      | any        -> return cv_student_pass
    end case
end function

(* ---- Concrete channel types ---- *)
channel Ch_Navigate is (r : Route)                         end channel
channel Ch_TypeInto is (sel : Selector, val : ConcreteValue) end channel
channel Ch_Click    is (sel : Selector)                    end channel
channel Ch_WaitFor  is (sel : Selector)                    end channel
channel Ch_Observe  is (sel : Selector)                    end channel

end module
